    content: "# Cell Theory and Scale\n\n*Grade 10 Biology & Chemistry · Unit 2 of 10 · Cells · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** state cell theory and reason about microscopic scale humbly.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models + evidence (CER). Prefer labeled diagrams over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWrite one question a scientist could ask that today's idea (“Cell Theory and Scale”) might help answer. Star what you are least sure about.\n\nShare: What prior idea from Unit 2 (or earlier) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nCells are systems with membranes and compartments that enable life processes. Today's skill — **Cell Theory and Scale** — sits inside **Cells**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nCell models explain health, growth, and later genetics. At Prosper Prep we connect science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show a model, cite evidence, revise when data conflicts.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **organelle** — define it and attach an example.\n- **membrane** — define it and attach an example.\n- **diffusion** — define it and attach an example.\n- **mitosis** — define it and attach an example.\n\n### Worked example A\n\n**Step 1 — Restate** the question for “Cell Theory and Scale.”\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation** (particle sketch, organelle map, Punnett, food web, balanced equation, feedback loop).\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea** with labeled evidence.\n**Step 4 — Check** against common mistakes such as: Memorizing organelle lists with no function, or drawing cells with no scale honesty.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined key terms, drew a labeled model, cited an observation, and revised when my first explanation contradicted conservation/selection/evidence rules.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nChange the context but keep the scientific structure. Write a claim a skeptic could test.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Memorizing organelle lists with no function, or drawing cells with no scale honesty.\n- **Also watch for:** unlabeled diagrams; treating models as perfect miniatures; slogan explanations.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step; redo from there.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nInquiry, cells, and energy set up genetics and ecology.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. Restate today's goal and list two measurable observations you would collect.\n2. Use two language-bank terms correctly in lab-notebook sentences.\n3. Error hunt: rewrite a slogan-only explanation into a CER.\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with models + evidence.\n\n1. Practice 1: A cell model shows a membrane separating inside/outside. Explain one job of the membrane and predict what happens if a channel protein for nutrient X stops working. Use numbers only if helpful (relative rates 6:3).\n2. Practice 2: A cell model shows a membrane separating inside/outside. Explain one job of the membrane and predict what happens if a channel protein for nutrient X stops working. Use numbers only if helpful (relative rates 3:0).\n3. Practice 3: A cell model shows a membrane separating inside/outside. Explain one job of the membrane and predict what happens if a channel protein for nutrient X stops working. Use numbers only if helpful (relative rates 4:2).\n4. Practice 4: A cell model shows a membrane separating inside/outside. Explain one job of the membrane and predict what happens if a channel protein for nutrient X stops working. Use numbers only if helpful (relative rates 7:-1).\n5. Practice 5: A cell model shows a membrane separating inside/outside. Explain one job of the membrane and predict what happens if a channel protein for nutrient X stops working. Use numbers only if helpful (relative rates 2:2).\n6. Practice 6: A cell model shows a membrane separating inside/outside. Explain one job of the membrane and predict what happens if a channel protein for nutrient X stops working. Use numbers only if helpful (relative rates 5:-1).\n\n### Answer key (quality bar)\n\n*Criteria for strong responses:*\n\n1. Membrane controls passage; blocked channels reduce uptake of X; evidence should mention selective permeability, not “cells get sad.”\n2. Membrane controls passage; blocked channels reduce uptake of X; evidence should mention selective permeability, not “cells get sad.”\n3. Membrane controls passage; blocked channels reduce uptake of X; evidence should mention selective permeability, not “cells get sad.”\n4. Membrane controls passage; blocked channels reduce uptake of X; evidence should mention selective permeability, not “cells get sad.”\n5. Membrane controls passage; blocked channels reduce uptake of X; evidence should mention selective permeability, not “cells get sad.”\n6. Membrane controls passage; blocked channels reduce uptake of X; evidence should mention selective permeability, not “cells get sad.”\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. What does “Cell Theory and Scale” help you explain more clearly now?\n2. Name one misconception and the repair.\n3. Sketch a tiny model (even stick-figure science) that belongs in your notes.\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 science is **model + evidence + revision**. Next up in Unit 2: Cells.\n",
