    content: "# Energy Unit CER Task\n\n*Grade 10 Biology & Chemistry · Unit 3 of 10 · Energy in Living Systems · Lesson 8*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** argue where energy goes in a closed classroom demo using evidence.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models + evidence (CER). Prefer labeled diagrams over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWrite one question a scientist could ask that today's idea (“Energy Unit CER Task”) might help answer. Star what you are least sure about.\n\nShare: What prior idea from Unit 3 (or earlier) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nEnergy transforms; it is not created from nowhere in biological systems. Today's skill — **Energy Unit CER Task** — sits inside **Energy in Living Systems**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nPhotosynthesis and respiration are the energy spine of ecology and physiology. At Prosper Prep we connect science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show a model, cite evidence, revise when data conflicts.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **ATP** — define it and attach an example.\n- **input** — define it and attach an example.\n- **output** — define it and attach an example.\n- **aerobic** — define it and attach an example.\n\n### Worked example A\n\n**Step 1 — Restate** the question for “Energy Unit CER Task.”\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation** (particle sketch, organelle map, Punnett, food web, balanced equation, feedback loop).\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea** with labeled evidence.\n**Step 4 — Check** against common mistakes such as: Saying plants don't respire, or treating energy as a substance that can be used up to zero without transfer.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined key terms, drew a labeled model, cited an observation, and revised when my first explanation contradicted conservation/selection/evidence rules.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nChange the context but keep the scientific structure. Write a claim a skeptic could test.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Saying plants don't respire, or treating energy as a substance that can be used up to zero without transfer.\n- **Also watch for:** unlabeled diagrams; treating models as perfect miniatures; slogan explanations.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step; redo from there.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nInquiry, cells, and energy set up genetics and ecology.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. Restate today's goal and list two measurable observations you would collect.\n2. Use two language-bank terms correctly in lab-notebook sentences.\n3. Error hunt: rewrite a slogan-only explanation into a CER.\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with models + evidence.\n\n1. Practice 1: Compare inputs/outputs for the process in “Energy Unit CER Task.” Identify where energy is stored or released, and name one measurement you would collect in a lab (10 minutes trial).\n2. Practice 2: Compare inputs/outputs for the process in “Energy Unit CER Task.” Identify where energy is stored or released, and name one measurement you would collect in a lab (27 minutes trial).\n3. Practice 3: Compare inputs/outputs for the process in “Energy Unit CER Task.” Identify where energy is stored or released, and name one measurement you would collect in a lab (48 minutes trial).\n4. Practice 4: Compare inputs/outputs for the process in “Energy Unit CER Task.” Identify where energy is stored or released, and name one measurement you would collect in a lab (25 minutes trial).\n5. Practice 5: Compare inputs/outputs for the process in “Energy Unit CER Task.” Identify where energy is stored or released, and name one measurement you would collect in a lab (46 minutes trial).\n6. Practice 6: Compare inputs/outputs for the process in “Energy Unit CER Task.” Identify where energy is stored or released, and name one measurement you would collect in a lab (23 minutes trial).\n\n### Answer key (quality bar)\n\n*Criteria for strong responses:*\n\n1. Correct inputs/outputs; energy location named (bonds/ATP/light); measurement is observable (gas, mass, temperature proxy) with controls.\n2. Correct inputs/outputs; energy location named (bonds/ATP/light); measurement is observable (gas, mass, temperature proxy) with controls.\n3. Correct inputs/outputs; energy location named (bonds/ATP/light); measurement is observable (gas, mass, temperature proxy) with controls.\n4. Correct inputs/outputs; energy location named (bonds/ATP/light); measurement is observable (gas, mass, temperature proxy) with controls.\n5. Correct inputs/outputs; energy location named (bonds/ATP/light); measurement is observable (gas, mass, temperature proxy) with controls.\n6. Correct inputs/outputs; energy location named (bonds/ATP/light); measurement is observable (gas, mass, temperature proxy) with controls.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. What does “Energy Unit CER Task” help you explain more clearly now?\n2. Name one misconception and the repair.\n3. Sketch a tiny model (even stick-figure science) that belongs in your notes.\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 science is **model + evidence + revision**. Next up in Unit 3: Energy in Living Systems.\n",
