    objectives: "• Read a simple tree as a hypothesis of relatedness.\n• Explain with a labeled model or data table.\n• Cite evidence and revise misconceptions.",
    content: "# Phylogenetic Thinking\n\n*Grade 10 Biology & Chemistry · Unit 5 of 10 · Evolution & Diversity · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** read a simple tree as a hypothesis of relatedness.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models + evidence (CER). Prefer labeled diagrams over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWrite one question a scientist could ask that today's idea (“Phylogenetic Thinking”) might help answer. Star what you are least sure about.\n\nShare: What prior idea from Unit 5 (or earlier) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nEvolution is a population process driven by selection on heritable variation. Today's skill — **Phylogenetic Thinking** — sits inside **Evolution & Diversity**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nModern biology is evolutionary; misconceptions block later learning. At Prosper Prep we connect science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show a model, cite evidence, revise when data conflicts.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **variation** — define it and attach an example.\n- **selection** — define it and attach an example.\n- **fitness** — define it and attach an example.\n- **speciation** — define it and attach an example.\n\n### Worked example A\n\n**Step 1 — Restate** the question for “Phylogenetic Thinking.”\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation** (particle sketch, organelle map, Punnett, food web, balanced equation, feedback loop).\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea** with labeled evidence.\n**Step 4 — Check** against common mistakes such as: Saying individuals evolve on purpose, or treating 'theory' as a wild guess.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined key terms, drew a labeled model, cited an observation, and revised when my first explanation contradicted conservation/selection/evidence rules.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nChange the context but keep the scientific structure. Write a claim a skeptic could test.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Saying individuals evolve on purpose, or treating 'theory' as a wild guess.\n- **Also watch for:** unlabeled diagrams; treating models as perfect miniatures; slogan explanations.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step; redo from there.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nGenetics and evolution explain diversity; ecology places organisms in systems.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. Restate today's goal and list two measurable observations you would collect.\n2. Use two language-bank terms correctly in lab-notebook sentences.\n3. Error hunt: rewrite a slogan-only explanation into a CER.\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with models + evidence.\n\n1. Practice 1: A trait shows 4 dominant : 4 recessive in a simple Mendelian cross prediction discussion. Explain what the ratio claims — and one reason a real pedigree might differ.\n2. Practice 2: A trait shows 7 dominant : 6 recessive in a simple Mendelian cross prediction discussion. Explain what the ratio claims — and one reason a real pedigree might differ.\n3. Practice 3: A trait shows 2 dominant : 3 recessive in a simple Mendelian cross prediction discussion. Explain what the ratio claims — and one reason a real pedigree might differ.\n4. Practice 4: A trait shows 5 dominant : 5 recessive in a simple Mendelian cross prediction discussion. Explain what the ratio claims — and one reason a real pedigree might differ.\n5. Practice 5: A trait shows 6 dominant : 3 recessive in a simple Mendelian cross prediction discussion. Explain what the ratio claims — and one reason a real pedigree might differ.\n6. Practice 6: A trait shows 3 dominant : 5 recessive in a simple Mendelian cross prediction discussion. Explain what the ratio claims — and one reason a real pedigree might differ.\n\n### Answer key (quality bar)\n\n*Criteria for strong responses:*\n\n1. Ratio is a probability model for large samples; real families are small, traits can be polygenic, environment can matter.\n2. Ratio is a probability model for large samples; real families are small, traits can be polygenic, environment can matter.\n3. Ratio is a probability model for large samples; real families are small, traits can be polygenic, environment can matter.\n4. Ratio is a probability model for large samples; real families are small, traits can be polygenic, environment can matter.\n5. Ratio is a probability model for large samples; real families are small, traits can be polygenic, environment can matter.\n6. Ratio is a probability model for large samples; real families are small, traits can be polygenic, environment can matter.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. What does “Phylogenetic Thinking” help you explain more clearly now?\n2. Name one misconception and the repair.\n3. Sketch a tiny model (even stick-figure science) that belongs in your notes.\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 science is **model + evidence + revision**. Next up in Unit 5: Evolution & Diversity.\n",
