    objectives: "• Model population limits with resources and competition.\n• Explain with a labeled model or data table.\n• Cite evidence and revise misconceptions.",
    content: "# Populations and Carrying Capacity\n\n*Grade 10 Biology & Chemistry · Unit 6 of 10 · Ecology & Human Impact · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** model population limits with resources and competition.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models + evidence (CER). Prefer labeled diagrams over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWrite one question a scientist could ask that today's idea (“Populations and Carrying Capacity”) might help answer. Star what you are least sure about.\n\nShare: What prior idea from Unit 6 (or earlier) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nEcosystems exchange energy and cycle matter; human choices have measurable effects. Today's skill — **Populations and Carrying Capacity** — sits inside **Ecology & Human Impact**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nStewardship requires data and tradeoffs, not slogans. At Prosper Prep we connect science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show a model, cite evidence, revise when data conflicts.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **carrying capacity** — define it and attach an example.\n- **trophic** — define it and attach an example.\n- **carbon cycle** — define it and attach an example.\n- **biodiversity** — define it and attach an example.\n\n### Worked example A\n\n**Step 1 — Restate** the question for “Populations and Carrying Capacity.”\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation** (particle sketch, organelle map, Punnett, food web, balanced equation, feedback loop).\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea** with labeled evidence.\n**Step 4 — Check** against common mistakes such as: Drawing food chains with arrows the wrong way, or claiming energy is recycled like matter.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined key terms, drew a labeled model, cited an observation, and revised when my first explanation contradicted conservation/selection/evidence rules.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nChange the context but keep the scientific structure. Write a claim a skeptic could test.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Drawing food chains with arrows the wrong way, or claiming energy is recycled like matter.\n- **Also watch for:** unlabeled diagrams; treating models as perfect miniatures; slogan explanations.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step; redo from there.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nGenetics and evolution explain diversity; ecology places organisms in systems.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. Restate today's goal and list two measurable observations you would collect.\n2. Use two language-bank terms correctly in lab-notebook sentences.\n3. Error hunt: rewrite a slogan-only explanation into a CER.\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with models + evidence.\n\n1. Practice 1: Sketch a mini food web with 4 organisms. Remove one species and predict two ripple effects. Cite energy-flow direction.\n2. Practice 2: Sketch a mini food web with 4 organisms. Remove one species and predict two ripple effects. Cite energy-flow direction.\n3. Practice 3: Sketch a mini food web with 4 organisms. Remove one species and predict two ripple effects. Cite energy-flow direction.\n4. Practice 4: Sketch a mini food web with 4 organisms. Remove one species and predict two ripple effects. Cite energy-flow direction.\n5. Practice 5: Sketch a mini food web with 4 organisms. Remove one species and predict two ripple effects. Cite energy-flow direction.\n6. Practice 6: Sketch a mini food web with 4 organisms. Remove one species and predict two ripple effects. Cite energy-flow direction.\n\n### Answer key (quality bar)\n\n*Criteria for strong responses:*\n\n1. Arrows point toward consumers; removals cause cascading effects; energy transfer is inefficient (not recycled 100% as usable energy).\n2. Arrows point toward consumers; removals cause cascading effects; energy transfer is inefficient (not recycled 100% as usable energy).\n3. Arrows point toward consumers; removals cause cascading effects; energy transfer is inefficient (not recycled 100% as usable energy).\n4. Arrows point toward consumers; removals cause cascading effects; energy transfer is inefficient (not recycled 100% as usable energy).\n5. Arrows point toward consumers; removals cause cascading effects; energy transfer is inefficient (not recycled 100% as usable energy).\n6. Arrows point toward consumers; removals cause cascading effects; energy transfer is inefficient (not recycled 100% as usable energy).\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. What does “Populations and Carrying Capacity” help you explain more clearly now?\n2. Name one misconception and the repair.\n3. Sketch a tiny model (even stick-figure science) that belongs in your notes.\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 science is **model + evidence + revision**. Next up in Unit 6: Ecology & Human Impact.\n",
