    content: "# Reactions Unit Lab CER\n\n*Grade 10 Biology & Chemistry · Unit 8 of 10 · Chemical Reactions & Quantities · Lesson 8*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** design or analyze a reaction investigation with quantitative claims.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models + evidence (CER). Prefer labeled diagrams over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWrite one question a scientist could ask that today's idea (“Reactions Unit Lab CER”) might help answer. Star what you are least sure about.\n\nShare: What prior idea from Unit 8 (or earlier) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nBalanced equations unlock quantitative predictions about reactants and products. Today's skill — **Reactions Unit Lab CER** — sits inside **Chemical Reactions & Quantities**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nLab and industry reasoning is stoichiometric at heart. At Prosper Prep we connect science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show a model, cite evidence, revise when data conflicts.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **coefficient** — define it and attach an example.\n- **mole ratio** — define it and attach an example.\n- **limiting reactant** — define it and attach an example.\n- **pH** — define it and attach an example.\n\n### Worked example A\n\n**Step 1 — Restate** the question for “Reactions Unit Lab CER.”\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation** (particle sketch, organelle map, Punnett, food web, balanced equation, feedback loop).\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea** with labeled evidence.\n**Step 4 — Check** against common mistakes such as: Skipping atom inventories, or confusing concentration language with 'strength' slogans.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined key terms, drew a labeled model, cited an observation, and revised when my first explanation contradicted conservation/selection/evidence rules.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nChange the context but keep the scientific structure. Write a claim a skeptic could test.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Skipping atom inventories, or confusing concentration language with 'strength' slogans.\n- **Also watch for:** unlabeled diagrams; treating models as perfect miniatures; slogan explanations.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step; redo from there.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nChemistry quantitative tools and body systems integrate the living/nonliving interface.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. Restate today's goal and list two measurable observations you would collect.\n2. Use two language-bank terms correctly in lab-notebook sentences.\n3. Error hunt: rewrite a slogan-only explanation into a CER.\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with models + evidence.\n\n1. Practice 1: For a simple reaction context tied to “Reactions Unit Lab CER,” balance a skeleton equation idea (conceptual counts 7 and 1) and state what is conserved.\n2. Practice 2: For a simple reaction context tied to “Reactions Unit Lab CER,” balance a skeleton equation idea (conceptual counts 6 and 3) and state what is conserved.\n3. Practice 3: For a simple reaction context tied to “Reactions Unit Lab CER,” balance a skeleton equation idea (conceptual counts 5 and 1) and state what is conserved.\n4. Practice 4: For a simple reaction context tied to “Reactions Unit Lab CER,” balance a skeleton equation idea (conceptual counts 4 and 3) and state what is conserved.\n5. Practice 5: For a simple reaction context tied to “Reactions Unit Lab CER,” balance a skeleton equation idea (conceptual counts 3 and 0) and state what is conserved.\n6. Practice 6: For a simple reaction context tied to “Reactions Unit Lab CER,” balance a skeleton equation idea (conceptual counts 2 and 3) and state what is conserved.\n\n### Answer key (quality bar)\n\n*Criteria for strong responses:*\n\n1. Atoms conserved; coefficients balance; mass conserved in a closed system; students show atom inventory.\n2. Atoms conserved; coefficients balance; mass conserved in a closed system; students show atom inventory.\n3. Atoms conserved; coefficients balance; mass conserved in a closed system; students show atom inventory.\n4. Atoms conserved; coefficients balance; mass conserved in a closed system; students show atom inventory.\n5. Atoms conserved; coefficients balance; mass conserved in a closed system; students show atom inventory.\n6. Atoms conserved; coefficients balance; mass conserved in a closed system; students show atom inventory.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. What does “Reactions Unit Lab CER” help you explain more clearly now?\n2. Name one misconception and the repair.\n3. Sketch a tiny model (even stick-figure science) that belongs in your notes.\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 science is **model + evidence + revision**. Next up in Unit 8: Chemical Reactions & Quantities.\n",
