    content: "# Capstone CER Portfolio\n\n*Grade 10 Biology & Chemistry · Unit 10 of 10 · Integrated Science Capstone · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** curate two polished CER paragraphs from earlier units with revision notes.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models + evidence (CER). Prefer labeled diagrams over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWrite one question a scientist could ask that today's idea (“Capstone CER Portfolio”) might help answer. Star what you are least sure about.\n\nShare: What prior idea from Unit 10 (or earlier) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nIntegrated science means one phenomenon, multiple models, stated limits. Today's skill — **Capstone CER Portfolio** — sits inside **Integrated Science Capstone**.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nCapstone habits mirror college lab reports and scientific citizenship. At Prosper Prep we connect science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show a model, cite evidence, revise when data conflicts.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **systems map** — define it and attach an example.\n- **limitation** — define it and attach an example.\n- **repeatability** — define it and attach an example.\n- **overclaim** — define it and attach an example.\n\n### Worked example A\n\n**Step 1 — Restate** the question for “Capstone CER Portfolio.”\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation** (particle sketch, organelle map, Punnett, food web, balanced equation, feedback loop).\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea** with labeled evidence.\n**Step 4 — Check** against common mistakes such as: Cherry-picking one confirming fact, or hiding uncertainty.\n\n**Sample narrative:** “I defined key terms, drew a labeled model, cited an observation, and revised when my first explanation contradicted conservation/selection/evidence rules.”\n\n### Worked example B\n\nChange the context but keep the scientific structure. Write a claim a skeptic could test.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** Cherry-picking one confirming fact, or hiding uncertainty.\n- **Also watch for:** unlabeled diagrams; treating models as perfect miniatures; slogan explanations.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step; redo from there.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nChemistry quantitative tools and body systems integrate the living/nonliving interface.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. Restate today's goal and list two measurable observations you would collect.\n2. Use two language-bank terms correctly in lab-notebook sentences.\n3. Error hunt: rewrite a slogan-only explanation into a CER.\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with models + evidence.\n\n1. Practice 1: Write a CER (claim-evidence-reasoning) paragraph applying “Capstone CER Portfolio” to a school-lab or everyday phenomenon. Include one quantitative or comparative observation.\n2. Practice 2: Write a CER (claim-evidence-reasoning) paragraph applying “Capstone CER Portfolio” to a school-lab or everyday phenomenon. Include one quantitative or comparative observation.\n3. Practice 3: Write a CER (claim-evidence-reasoning) paragraph applying “Capstone CER Portfolio” to a school-lab or everyday phenomenon. Include one quantitative or comparative observation.\n4. Practice 4: Write a CER (claim-evidence-reasoning) paragraph applying “Capstone CER Portfolio” to a school-lab or everyday phenomenon. Include one quantitative or comparative observation.\n5. Practice 5: Write a CER (claim-evidence-reasoning) paragraph applying “Capstone CER Portfolio” to a school-lab or everyday phenomenon. Include one quantitative or comparative observation.\n6. Practice 6: Write a CER (claim-evidence-reasoning) paragraph applying “Capstone CER Portfolio” to a school-lab or everyday phenomenon. Include one quantitative or comparative observation.\n\n### Answer key (quality bar)\n\n*Criteria for strong responses:*\n\n1. Claim is specific; evidence is observable; reasoning links to the unit model without slogans.\n2. Claim is specific; evidence is observable; reasoning links to the unit model without slogans.\n3. Claim is specific; evidence is observable; reasoning links to the unit model without slogans.\n4. Claim is specific; evidence is observable; reasoning links to the unit model without slogans.\n5. Claim is specific; evidence is observable; reasoning links to the unit model without slogans.\n6. Claim is specific; evidence is observable; reasoning links to the unit model without slogans.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. What does “Capstone CER Portfolio” help you explain more clearly now?\n2. Name one misconception and the repair.\n3. Sketch a tiny model (even stick-figure science) that belongs in your notes.\n\n## Wrap-up\n\nStrong Grade 10 science is **model + evidence + revision**. Next up in Unit 10: Integrated Science Capstone.\n",
